/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - Direction needs a reference.
   - A bearing is the angle between a survey line and a stated meridian, and may be written whole circle or reduced.
   - A whole circle bearing (WCB) is measured clockwise from north, from 0° to 360°.
   - θ is the whole circle bearing in degrees; NE means 0° < θ < 90°, SE means 90° < θ < 180°, SW means 180° < θ < 270°, NW means 270° < θ < 360°.
   - RB is the reduced bearing in degrees from 0° to 90°, with the quadrant letters chosen first.
   - The rule is mechanical.
   - The fore bearing (FB) is measured from the station to the next point.
   - An interior angle is inside the closed figure; a deflection angle is measured from the prolongation of the previous line to the next line, left or right.
   - Declination changes slowly with time, so maps state its year.
*/
