/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - A compass needle is a small magnet on a sharp pivot.
   - The plain (surveyor's) compass fixes the ring to the box and moves the needle inside it; you read quadrantal bearings, 0° to 90°.
   - Temporary adjustments, repeated at every station:
   - A compass traverse measures the bearing of each line in turn.
   - At each station read two bearings: the fore bearing (FB) of the line ahead, and the back bearing (BB) of the line behind.
   - FB is the fore bearing at the station, in degrees clockwise from magnetic north; BB is the back bearing of the same line at the far station; use + when BB < 180° (answer stays below 360°) and − when BB > 180°.
   - The second check works only on a closed polygon and uses the interior angles.
   - n is the number of sides of the closed polygon and the sum is in degrees: 180° for a triangle, 360° for a quadrilateral, 540° for a pentagon.
   - An interior angle at a station comes from the two bearings there: take their difference, and choose the value inside the polygon.
   - The needle obeys every magnetic field, not only the earth's.
*/
